          <Pad Id="JDdjjdLRhiLEQVcahS7xJh" Bounds="-620,-60,540,1150" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="05 - Grow a shape.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;One Distance knob, three geometries. Drag it and a point becomes a disc, a path becomes&#xD;&#xA;a corridor, a plot grows a margin - all three at once, because they all drink from the&#xD;&#xA;same number. Geometry that MAKES geometry: this is the chapter where GIS stops being&#xD;&#xA;only analysis and starts being a material.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;DRAG IT NEGATIVE. The plot erodes inward. The point and the path simply vanish - a&#xD;&#xA;thing with no interior has nothing to erode, and NTS answers with an EMPTY geometry,&#xD;&#xA;not an error. The Is Empty box is watching.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;SET SEGMENTS TO 3. The disc becomes a triangle-ish blob. There is no circle - there&#xD;&#xA;never was. A buffered point is 4 x Segments straight edges pretending, and now you can&#xD;&#xA;see the pretence. 8 is NetTopologySuite's own default.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE LOOP. A buffer's vertex count changes with every knob twitch, so no fixed wiring&#xD;&#xA;can draw it. The ForEach region takes however many Coordinates arrive and hands back&#xD;&#xA;that many Vector2s - the bridge from chapter 04, now crossing a whole crowd at once.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Wire an LFO through a Map node into Distance and watch all three breathe. Then&#xD;&#xA;  replace the LFO with anything - audio level, OSC, a sensor. A GIS operation driven&#xD;&#xA;  by a live signal is the whole reason this course exists in vvvv and not in a&#xD;&#xA;  desktop GIS.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Buffer the buffer: chain a second Buffer node after the first, with its own small&#xD;&#xA;  Distance. Rounded corners on everything.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Move the path's middle vertex while Distance is large: the corridor heals itself&#xD;&#xA;  around every bend.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  Buffer is the first node you have met that RETURNS NEW GEOMETRY rather than a number&#xD;&#xA;  or a yes/no - and it composes: its output feeds Area, Contains, Distance, or another&#xD;&#xA;  Buffer. Every operation from here on has that shape. Units are still this canvas's&#xD;&#xA;  own; on the earth a buffer in real metres needs a metric reference system first,&#xD;&#xA;  which is a later chapter's whole story.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  buffer            the region within a distance of a geometry&#xD;&#xA;  negative buffer   erosion - the same idea, inward&#xD;&#xA;  segments          how many straight edges approximate each quarter turn&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  Dilate and erode from image morphology, the offset/outline tool from vector design,&#xD;&#xA;  an SDF isoline - all the same idea, and you already own all three.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.NetTopologySuite, which is normally a reason to live in&#xD;&#xA;that package's own help folder rather than here. It is the exemption written into this&#xD;&#xA;pack's rules: the geometry act runs on that package alone, and a course with a hole in&#xD;&#xA;its opening act is worse than a node appearing in two places.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - the geometry act ends here. Three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 06             the earth arrives - and these five chapters come with you&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt Grow a town         geometry that generates geometry, taken to a whole town&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 03             ask your grown shapes how they relate">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
        </Canvas>
